      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine rice and lentils in a pot with 1.2 litres water, turmeric, ginger, and salt. Bring to a boil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Skim any foam. Reduce heat to low, cover, and cook 25 minutes, stirring occasionally, until both rice and lentils are very soft and the mixture is porridge-like."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Make the tempering: heat ghee in a small pan until shimmering. Add cumin seeds, bay leaves, cloves, asafoetida, and dried chilli. Fry 1 minute."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the tempered ghee over the khichdi. Stir gently. Top with coriander and serve with plain yoghurt and papad."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Khichdi is too stiff",
              "cause": "Not enough water",
              "fix": "Stir in 150 ml hot water and cook on low for another 5 minutes."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Lentils are grainy",
              "cause": "Undercooked",
              "fix": "Add more water and continue cooking, covered, until both lentils and rice are completely soft."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Khichdi is eaten across India in dozens of regional variations. In Gujarat it is paired with kadhi, a yoghurt-based soup. In West Bengal it is made with a full set of vegetables and called khichuri. In the Ayurvedic tradition it is prescribed during illness and after surgery for its easy digestibility. The Mughal emperor Akbar is said to have been particularly fond of khichdi. A version with broth poured over it, called dal bati or dal baati, is common in Rajasthan."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
